/-!
# Compatible selection for the three-duad image-eight movers

In the surviving image-eight branch, the actual edge mover is one of the
three order-four natural offenders. For the mixed and even planes, two
short permutation identities construct a compatible edge and a single
order-three quotient transporter. The selected mover and two endpoint
transpositions generate the symmetric quotient; that same transporter
belongs to the subgroup generated by the mover and its conjugate.

Lifting through the entire quotient kernel gives residual generation with
the two full endpoint seeds and transporter membership in the actual edge
movers. Its lift has cube in the starting core; no split cover or order-three
lift is assumed. The paired trapping theorem, including its zero-commutator
case, then constructs an overlapping backward critical path. Pulling the
second orientation back retains every vertex of the original path.
Consequently trapped radii bound equal-mover slices at every component
vertex and incident edge.

The two-transposition mover alternative remains explicit. This does not
exclude it, supply unconditional reverse-mover equality or radius escape,
or prove the distance bounds. Both moved covers and all central attachments
remain in the literal replacement graph.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two
(1993), (5.6)--(5.7), printed pp.917--919; the order-four offender catalogue
is Aschbacher--Smith B.3.4, printed pp.84--85.
-/

public noncomputable section
open Subgroup Theory.GroupTheory
open CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.Offenders CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.Offenders.Six
open Theory.SpecificGroups.SymmetricSix
open scoped commutatorElement Pointwise
